FINDINGS.md
REPORT.md
# phase-044 — findings

Work that belongs to a later phase, with the evidence for it. Nothing here was
done under phase-044's name; none of it is on-topic for the manifest and
lockfile it was building.

## 1. `rb run`'s import of a pinned version still has no automated test

**Where:** `src/interpreter.rs:441` (`module_path`), `src/bytecode/vm.rs:580`
(`module_paths`), both of which consult `crate::manifest::pinned_module_path_in`
before the three existing search paths.

**What:** the *analyzer* half is now tested — `analyze_in(&program, &root)` takes
the directory the program is resolved from, so `tests/manifest_test.rs` drives a
real import against a real published module and pins both what it binds and what
a broken manifest says instead. The *loader* half — the `rb run` path that opens
the file the manifest named — is verified by hand (output quoted in `REPORT.md`)
and not by a test: `module_path` resolves against `Path::new(".")`, so an
automated test would have to call `set_current_dir`, and `cargo test` runs test
binaries in parallel threads sharing one process working directory. One such
test would make every other test in the binary depend on when it ran.

**What would fix it:** the same root parameter threaded from the CLI into the
loader, or a `Vfs`-level root so that the working directory is data rather than
process state. `src/vfs.rs` already exists for exactly this reason (it is why a
module load on wasm reads the in-memory filesystem) and is the obvious place.
Until then, a regression in the `rb run` wiring would not be caught by
`cargo test`.

## 2. Any resolve writes the lockfile, including one from the analyzer

**Where:** `src/manifest.rs` — `failure_in`, `pinned_module_path_in` and
`resolve_in` all go through the writing path, and `analyze_in` calls
`failure_in` once per `import`.

**What:** running `rb lint` or `rb format` over a program in a directory with a
`redblue.manifest` now creates `redblue.lock` beside it. That is what `cargo
check` does with a `Cargo.lock`, so it is defensible, but it was not a stated
consequence of this phase and it is not obvious from the function names: nothing
in `failure_in` or in `analyze_in` says it writes.

**What would fix it:** a read-only `resolve_without_writing`, used by the
analyzer, so that a static pass answers questions without touching the
filesystem beyond reading it.

