 ## What changed
 
+Two runs produced this phase. The first built the manifest, the resolver and the
+lockfile. This run finished it: the resolver's failures were being reported, but
+the program the user was looking at was never told.
+
 | File | Lines | What |
 |---|---|---|
-| `src/manifest.rs` | +656 | New. `Version`, `Requirement`, `Manifest`, the `Registry` trait, `Resolution`, `resolve`, `resolve_in`, `DirRegistry`, `pinned_module_path` |
-| `src/lib.rs` | +1 | `pub mod manifest;` |
-| `src/interpreter.rs` | +43 −18 | `module_search_paths` asks the manifest first and returns `Result`; `module_path` skips a directory |
-| `src/bytecode/vm.rs` | +28 −11 | `module_paths` mirrors both, so both VMs resolve an import identically |
-| `tests/manifest_test.rs` | +525 | 20 new `#[test]`s |
+| `src/manifest.rs` | +656 | *first run.* `Version`, `Requirement`, `Manifest`, the `Registry` trait, `Resolution`, `resolve`, `resolve_in`, `DirRegistry`, `pinned_module_path` |
+| `src/lib.rs` | +1 | *first run.* `pub mod manifest;` |
+| `src/interpreter.rs` | +43 −18 | *first run.* the manifest first in the import search; a directory is skipped, not opened |
+| `src/bytecode/vm.rs` | +28 −11 | *first run.* the same search in the bytecode VM, so both engines resolve an import identically |
+| `src/manifest.rs` | +26 | *this run.* `pinned_module_path_in` and `failure_in` — the same two answers, asked of a named directory rather than of the working directory |
+| `src/interpreter.rs` | +21 −23 | *this run.* `module_search_paths`, `module_source_in` and `module_bound_names_in` take that directory, so the analyzer reads the pins of the program it is asked about |
+| `src/analyzer.rs` | +77 −12 | *this run.* `analyze_in`; `Analyzer` holds a root; an `import` whose manifest cannot be resolved is the failure the walk stops for |
+| `tests/manifest_test.rs` | +525, then +129 | 20 `#[test]`s, then 5 more |
+
+### The gap this run closed
+
+The first run's claim was "the resolution error reaches the program through
+`import`". It reached the program only when the program read nothing from the
+module. A program that did read from it got this instead:
+
+```
